{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 63, :comments ["<b>התרשתא</b> — Some say: he was so called because drinking of gentile wine was permitted to him, and the meaning is \"the permitted drank\" [hutir shata]. It is also possible: that it is a name of splendor and greatness in the Chaldean language, like the sense of sar (governor) or pechah (satrap), and this is the correct interpretation." "<b>הקדש הקדשים</b> — These are the terumot." "<b>עד עמוד כהן לאורים ותומים</b> — The Urim that Moses made were hidden away, and no others were made after them. And the meaning of \"until a priest stands\" — one fit for the Urim and the Thummim."], :book :ezra, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The folk etymology 'hutir shata' is Ibn Ezra's own explanation of what 'some say'; rendered literally as he gives it."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Ezra -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/ezra"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:41:44.092307500Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}